course: biology
lesson: 318
title: Deer: antlers that fall off and grow again; red deer, roe deer, moose, muntjac and water deer
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Imagine growing a pair of bony branches on your head every summer, fighting with them in autumn and dropping them in winter. That is what a stag does with its antlers, every single year.
explain: Antlers are pure bone and among the fastest-growing bone of any mammal. While they grow they are covered in soft furry velvet full of blood; then the velvet dries and the deer rubs it off on trees.
explain: Horns are different: a cow or goat keeps its horns for life, a bone core inside a cover of keratin. So deer have antlers, not horns.
explain: Deer come in all sizes, from the moose, taller than a grown man at the shoulder, to the little muntjac that barks like a dog in English woods at night.
fact: Deer antlers fall off every year and grow back; they are bone and can grow over 2 centimetres a day in big deer, and fallen antlers are often gnawed by mice and squirrels for their minerals.
fact: Growing antlers are covered in velvet, a skin full of blood vessels; when the antlers are fully grown the velvet dries and the deer rubs it off on trees, and scientists study antlers to learn how bone can regrow fast.
fact: Horns, like those of cows and goats, have a bone core covered with keratin and stay for life; the pronghorn of America sheds the outer cover of its horns every year, a bit of both.
fact: Each tip of an antler is called a point or tine; a big red deer stag can have twelve or more, and hunters called a twelve-pointer a royal, but points do not show a stag's exact age.
fact: In the autumn rut, red deer stags roar, walk side by side and sometimes lock antlers; roaring and walking show strength without a real fight, and the red deer is the biggest wild land mammal in Britain.
fact: Roe deer are small, about 70 centimetres at the shoulder, with a white rump; when alarmed they bark like a dog, and their spotted babies lie still alone in the grass while the mother feeds nearby.
fact: The moose, called elk in Europe, is the biggest deer, up to about 2 metres at the shoulder; it dives for water plants, can close its nostrils under water, and a bull's antlers can be about 1.8 metres across.
fact: Crashes with moose are dangerous because long legs put the heavy body at windscreen height; car makers in Sweden test cars with a sharp swerve called the moose test.
fact: The muntjac, a small deer from China, escaped from parks into the English countryside about a hundred years ago; males have short antlers and long tusk-like teeth, it barks, and it can breed at any time of year.
fact: The Chinese water deer has no antlers at all; males have long fangs instead, and it lives in reed beds and wet fields.
fact: The Chinese water deer is threatened in China, but escaped water deer spread in England, which now holds a big share of the world's population, reported.
fact: Six kinds of deer live wild in Britain: red, roe, fallow, sika, muntjac and Chinese water deer; only red and roe are native, the other four were brought by people.
fact: Reindeer are the only deer in which both males and females grow antlers; males drop theirs in early winter, while females keep theirs through the winter.
exercise: What happens to a deer's antlers every year? | They fall off and grow back | They grow one point longer and stay | They turn into horns | They are shed only once in a lifetime
exercise: What are antlers made of? | Bone | Keratin, like fingernails | Hair stuck together | Wood from trees
exercise: How fast can antlers grow in big deer? | Over 2 centimetres a day | About 2 centimetres a year | About 2 metres a day | They do not grow, they are born full size
exercise: What is the velvet on growing antlers? | A soft skin full of blood vessels | Moss that grows on them in the damp forest | Fur the deer glues on | A disease that kills the antlers
exercise: What does a deer do when its antlers have finished growing? | Rubs the dried velvet off on trees | Eats its velvet for lunch | Keeps the velvet for ever | Drops the antlers at once
exercise: How are horns different from antlers? | Horns have a bone core covered in keratin and stay for life | Horns fall off every year in spring, and antlers stay on for life | Horns are made of wood | There is no difference at all
exercise: What is each tip of an antler called? | A point or tine | A horn or hoof | A branch or twig | A tusk or fang
exercise: Can you tell a stag's exact age by counting its points? | No, more points often means a healthy grown stag but not an exact age | Yes, a stag grows exactly one new point on its antlers for each year of its life | Yes, two points for each year | No, all stags have the same number
exercise: Why do red deer stags roar and walk side by side in the rut? | To show their strength without a real fight | To keep warm on the cold, frosty autumn nights | To call their babies | To scare away wolves only
exercise: Which is the biggest wild land mammal in Britain? | The red deer | The roe deer | The fox | The badger
exercise: What does an alarmed roe deer do? | Barks like a dog | Roars like a lion | Hisses like a snake | Stays completely silent
exercise: Why should you never pick up a fawn lying alone in the grass? | Its mother is feeding nearby and will come back | It has been left behind on purpose and is waiting to be adopted | Fawns bite very hard | It is always ill
exercise: How does a moose eat water plants? | It dives and closes its nostrils under water | It sends its calf to fetch them | It drinks the lake dry | It uses its antlers like a net
exercise: Why are moose so dangerous to hit with a car? | Long legs put the heavy body at windscreen height | They see cars as rivals and run into them on purpose | Their antlers are made of steel | They are invisible at night
exercise: Where did the muntjac in English woods come from? | It escaped from parks, coming first from China | It has always lived in England | It swam from France | It came from Australia on ships
exercise: What do male Chinese water deer have instead of antlers? | Long fangs | Horns | Tusks on their noses | Nothing at all on the head or mouth
exercise: Which deer are native to Britain? | Red and roe deer | Muntjac and water deer | Sika and fallow deer | Moose and reindeer
exercise: In which deer do both males and females grow antlers? | Reindeer | Red deer | Roe deer | Muntjac
test: «found a deer horn in the woods!!» What is it more likely to be? | A shed antler; deer drop their antlers every year | A horn that fell off a cow that got lost in the woods | A deer's leg bone | A tooth from a moose
test: Count them: a royal stag has twelve points. How many points do 3 royal stags have together? | 36 | 24 | 12 | 15
test: Count them: a park has 5 red deer stags and 4 hinds. Only stags grow antlers. How many antlers are there in summer? | 10 | 18 | 9 | 8
test: Count them: 4 reindeer pull a sledge, 2 males and 2 females. In summer both sexes grow antlers. How many antlers are there? | 8 | 4 | 2 | 6
test: On a Christmas card Rudolph and the other reindeer pull Santa's sleigh in late December with big antlers. What do some people joke? | Males drop antlers in early winter, so they may be females or young ones | They must be red deer dressed up in reindeer costumes for the Christmas card | Only male reindeer grow antlers | Reindeer grow new antlers just for Christmas
test: In Bambi, the old stag has big antlers but Bambi's mother has none. Is the film right? | Yes, in most deer only males grow antlers | No, all female deer have antlers | No, no deer has antlers | Yes, but only because she lost them in the snow
test: «heard a dog barking in the woods at midnight, nobody's dog was out» What could it be? | A muntjac or a roe deer, which both bark | A wolf in disguise | A moose singing | A rabbit growling
test: «mate says it's a vampire deer cos it has fangs lol» What is it really? | A Chinese water deer, whose males have long fangs instead of antlers | A deer that creeps out at night and drinks the blood of sheep and cows | A muntjac with broken antlers | A cartoon animal that is not real
test: What would happen if a stag's antler velvet were badly torn while the antler was still growing? | It would bleed, because the velvet is full of blood vessels | Nothing at all, because velvet has no blood and no feeling in it | The antler would turn into a horn | The stag would stop growing for ever
test: Why would roaring and walking side by side be better for stags than fighting at once? | The weaker stag can see he would lose and leave unhurt | Roaring makes the antlers grow | Fighting is forbidden by the herd leader | They are too tired to fight in autumn
test: «why do car people in Sweden do a 'moose test'» What is the answer? | Moose crashes are dangerous, so cars are tested on a sharp swerve | They count how many moose they can spot from the car in one hour | They test if moose can drive | They paint moose on cars for luck
test: «only 2 of britain's 6 wild deer were here first?» Which four were brought by people? | Fallow, sika, muntjac and Chinese water deer | Red, roe, fallow and sika deer | Moose, reindeer, muntjac and roe deer | Red, sika, muntjac and Chinese water deer
exam: Identify the principal distinction between the antlers of deer and the horns of cattle. | Antlers consist of bone and are shed annually, whereas horns comprise a bony core sheathed in keratin and are retained for life | Antlers are retained throughout the life of the animal, whereas horns are shed annually and regrown each spring in a larger form | Antlers consist of keratin alone, whereas horns consist of wood | No distinction exists, the terms being synonymous
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reindeer. | It is the sole species of deer in which both sexes customarily bear antlers | It is a species of deer in which neither sex bears antlers | It is a species of goat bearing permanent horns | It is the sole species of deer native to Britain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the species of deer living wild in Britain? | Six species are present, of which only the red and the roe deer are native | Two species are present, both having been introduced by the Romans for hunting | Twelve species are present, all of which are native | One species is present, namely the moose
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a collision between a motor vehicle and a moose is especially hazardous. | The length of the animal's legs places its heavy body at the height of the windscreen | The animal regards vehicles as rival males during the rut and deliberately charges them | The antlers of the animal are composed of metal | The animal cannot be observed in daylight
